          "create_time": 1792282837.11,
          "update_time": 1792282838.896,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "There is a useful extension beyond the review: even a one-dimensional normalized perturbation space produces small, nondegenerate collisions inside sufficiently high zero gaps at \\(t=1/4\\). The proof uses the published bound \\(\\Lambda_\\theta\\le0.22\\) and a concavity argument. The finite tail calculation also confirms that a perturbation of recurrence norm \\(10^{-16}\\) removes every real zero above \\(x=269\\), uniformly for \\(0\\le t\\le1/4\\)."
            ]
          },
